    "message": {
     "content": "### The Bottom Line\n\n**Yes, you should seriously consider taking this offer.** \n\nWhile they refused a cash sign-on bonus, they compensated for it by pushing your equity to **$1.28M**, which puts this package at the **top 5\u201310% of Google\u2019s L6 PM compensation band**. \n\nFinancially, this package more than covers the $200,000 in unvested Meta RSUs you would walk away from over the next 12 months.\n\n---\n\n### The Financial Breakdown\n\nGoogle uses a front-loaded vesting schedule (typically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** over 4 years). Here is how your realized compensation will look compared to your current Meta run rate (~$526k/yr):\n\n| Year | Google Base | Google Target Bonus (20%) | Google Equity (Front-loaded) | **Google Total Comp** | **Meta Total Comp** | **Annual Delta** |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $422,400 (33%) | **~$710,400** | ~$526,000 | **+$184,400** |\n| **Year 2** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $422,400 (33%) | **~$710,400** *(+ Refresher)* | ~$526,000 | **+$184,400** |\n| **Year 3** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $281,600 (22%) | **~$569,600** *(+ 2 Refreshers)* | ~$526,000 | **+$43,600+** |\n| **Year 4** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $153,600 (12%) | **~$441,600** *(+ 3 Refreshers)* | ~$526,000 | *(Offset by stacked refreshers)* |\n\n#### Key Financial Takeaways:\n1. **The $200k \"Loss\" is Erased in Year 1:** You gain roughly **+$184k in Year 1** and another **+$184k in Year 2** over your current Meta compensation. Over the first two years alone, you will take home **~$368,000 more** than staying at Meta at your current rate.\n2. **Why Google Handled It This Way:** Google's Compensation Committee often prefers granting top-of-band equity over cash sign-ons for retention purposes. You successfully forced them to max out their equity band.\n\n---\n\n### Decision Matrix: Take It vs. Stay at Meta\n\nSince the compensation is mathematically a win, your decision comes down to career trajectory and culture:\n\n#### Reasons to Take the Offer:\n* **The \"FAANG Trifecta\" Resume Power:** Having both **Meta IC6** and **Google L6 PM** establishes you as an elite-tier senior product leader, opening up future Head of Product, VP, or early-stage CPO roles.\n* **Massive Upfront Cashflow:** Earning ~$710k/yr for the next two years provides immediate, derisked financial upside.\n* **Product Scope:** If the SF Google team's charter offers strong executive visibility or ownership over a strategic product area.\n\n#### Reasons to Stay at Meta:\n* **You are on an Immediate Track to IC7:** If you are within 6\u201312 months of an IC7 promotion at Meta, staying makes sense\u2014Meta IC7 comp often lands in the $800k\u2013$1M+ range. Starting over at Google means resetting your promotion clock.\n* **Pace & Culture Preference:** Meta is famously bottom-up, scrappy, and execution-heavy. Google PM culture is much more consensus-driven, matrixed, and stakeholder-heavy. If navigating slower organizational consensus frustrates you, staying at Meta may be better for day-to-day satisfaction.\n\n---\n\n### Recommendation\n\nUnless you are on the cusp of an IC7 promotion at Meta within the year, **accept the offer**. \n\nYou extracted almost $200,000 in additional equity from the initial offer, matched your base, and unlocked a **~$710k Year 1 & 2 run rate**. This is a top-bracket outcome for an L6 PM hire.",
